\title{Comparing BMAD and GitHub Spec Kit for Spec-Driven Development: A
Requirements Engineering Case Study on a Java Swing Calculator}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Varun Ganesh Sondur}
\IEEEauthorblockA{\textit{Tampere University}}
\and
\IEEEauthorblockN{Mohammadamin Lotfiourimi}
\IEEEauthorblockA{\textit{Tampere University}}
\and
\IEEEauthorblockN{Pham Thanh}
\IEEEauthorblockA{\textit{Tampere University}}
\and
\IEEEauthorblockN{Georgios Malezoglou}
\IEEEauthorblockA{\textit{Tampere University}}}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Spec-driven development (SDD) tools let coding agents work from written
specifications instead of chat instructions. We ask, through four
research questions measured against five Goal Question Metric goals, how
two such tools, BMAD and GitHub Spec Kit, support the step from
requirements to implementation and the handling of a requirement change.
Using a comparative case study, two team members built the same Java
Swing calculator, one with each tool, from one frozen set of
requirements (8 functional and 5 non-functional requirements, 19
acceptance criteria). The same change request, which adds square, square
root and reciprocal operations (11 requirements, 22 criteria), was then
given to both. Independent evaluators later ran common execution sheets.
Both workflows reported all 22 change criteria and all 19 baseline
re-checks as passed. The independent baseline evaluation gave BMAD 19
passes and Spec Kit 18 passes and one blocked check (an offline-launch
prerequisite), which is a difference in evidence, not in demonstrated
correctness. Spec Kit's independent change evaluation left more
inspectable raw evidence. Effort, change-impact recall, native
traceability and rubric ratings were not measured, so we make no claim
about an overall winner. The study has one case, one run per workflow
and confounded operator, agent and environment.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
spec-driven development, requirements engineering, traceability, change
impact analysis, BMAD, GitHub Spec Kit
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}\label{introduction}

Coding agents now produce working software from short instructions, but
the intent behind that output is easily lost when the instructions live
only in a chat \cite{agarwal2026}. Spec-driven development (SDD)
responds by making the specification an operational input: a person
writes or approves a specification, a plan and a task list, and the
agent generates code against them \cite{piskala2026, claudino2026}.
Tools such as GitHub Spec Kit \cite{speckit} and BMAD \cite{bmad}
package this idea into installable workflows.

From a requirements engineering (RE) point of view, the interesting
question is not which tool generates better code. It is how each
workflow carries requirements through specification, design, tasks, code
and tests, whether those links can be followed, what happens when a
requirement changes, and how much of the original requirement set the
result satisfies. The sources we read describe SDD practices and test
claims about them at repository scale
\cite{claudino2026, agarwal2026, hill2026}, but they do not compare two
named SDD tools on one controlled software case with a common change
request.

This report presents such a comparison as a small, bounded case study.
Two members of our team each operated one tool to build the same desktop
calculator from the same frozen requirements, then applied the same
change request. Two other members evaluated the results independently
with shared checklists. The study is descriptive: the unit of comparison
is the whole tool, agent and operator workflow, and we do not claim to
isolate the effect of either tool.

We make three contributions. First, we describe both tools as they were
installed and used, with their native artefacts and change paths.
Second, we report acceptance outcomes, change-impact observations and
process observations for the baseline and the change, and we state for
every number which evidence layer it comes from. Third, we list which
planned measures could not be calculated and why. The report first gives
the background and the method, which includes the research questions. It
then describes the tools, presents the results, discusses the questions
and the threats to validity, and ends with a conclusion and the AI-use
declaration.

\section{Background}\label{background}

\subsection{Spec-Driven Development}\label{spec-driven-development}

SDD treats the specification as the source of truth and the code as a
generated or verified secondary product. Piskala distinguishes three
levels of specification rigour: spec-first, where a specification is
written before the code; spec-anchored, where it is kept alongside the
code; and spec-as-source, where the specification is the main artefact
\cite{piskala2026}. We use this vocabulary only to describe the
workflows. We do not rank the tools by it; the abstract does not present
it as a measure for comparing tools.

Claudino et al.~scored specification, plan and task artefacts of 114
open-source SDD repositories against ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148 and derived a
framework of 40 practices in 10 quality dimensions \cite{claudino2026}.
Agarwal et al.~combined a thematic analysis, 21 practitioner interviews
and a corpus of 470,795 specification files from 266 repositories; they
report that implementation detail in specifications may help agents but
makes specifications more likely to go stale, and that specification and
code are usually checked against each other only when a change is
finished \cite{agarwal2026}. Hill analysed 88,052 pull requests in 119
repositories and found no meaningful difference in defect introduction
between specified and unspecified pull requests after matching; the
specification artefacts in that sample were mostly references to issues
and tickets, and the author notes that the effect of adopting SDD tools
remains untested \cite{hill2026}. We read only the abstract pages of
these three works, and two of them are preprints whose peer-review
status we have not established. They justify caution: a specification is
not automatically a quality improvement.

Vibe coding, meaning prompting an agent without a written specification,
is named in our project brief as a contrasting approach. It is not an
experimental condition here.

\subsection{Requirements Engineering Concepts
Used}\label{requirements-engineering-concepts-used}

We use four RE concepts as operational definitions. \emph{Traceability}
is the ability to follow a requirement forward to design, tasks, code
and tests and back. We separate links created by the workflow itself
(native links) from links reconstructed afterwards by an evaluator,
because the second kind says little about what the workflow supports.
\emph{Change-impact analysis} is the statement, made before coding, of
which requirements, design elements, code and tests a change will touch.
\emph{Acceptance criteria} are checks that turn a requirement into
something an evaluator can observe. \emph{Non-functional requirements}
(NFRs) are quality constraints such as launch behaviour or the absence
of network access; in a small GUI application they are the hardest to
turn into observable criteria.

\subsection{Related Work}\label{related-work}

We did not conduct a systematic literature search; the sources above
were found through general web searches and the reference lists of other
papers. Within them, the work closest to ours analyses repositories at
scale \cite{claudino2026, agarwal2026, hill2026}. Our study differs in
design: it is small, runs two named tools on one shared case, and
applies one requirement change to both. Its methodological basis is
guidance for software engineering case studies \cite{runeson2009} and
for measurement and data collection in empirical studies
\cite{kitchenham2002}. Measures are organised with the Goal Question
Metric approach \cite{basili1994}. We read the bibliographic records of
these three sources, not their full texts.

\section{Method}\label{method}

\subsection{Research Questions}\label{research-questions}

Our main question is: \emph{How do SDD tools support the transition from
requirements to implementation, and how well does the result match the
original requirements?} It is split into four research questions, taken
from the course brief (\texttt{ReadMe.md}). These four are the final set
for this report.

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{RQ1.} How does each tool create and structure specifications,
  including formats, levels of detail, templates and the role of the
  human?
\item
  \textbf{RQ2.} How are requirements turned into design, tasks, code and
  tests, and is the link between these artefacts traceable?
\item
  \textbf{RQ3.} How does each tool handle changes to the specification,
  and does the specification remain aligned with the code?
\item
  \textbf{RQ4.} How closely does the generated implementation satisfy
  the original requirements?
\end{itemize}

The team's comparison framework organises measurement differently, into
five Goal Question Metric goals (G1 to G5) with a catalogue of measures
\cite{basili1994}. The team's final analysis also phrased its five
questions as comparisons (``which workflow performed better\ldots{}'').
We keep RQ1 to RQ4 as the questions and use the goals as the measurement
structure. Table~\ref{tbl-rqmap} shows how they map. G4 (human
work) and G5 (evidence and reproducibility) have no matching course
question, so the report answers them as supporting goals in dedicated
sections of the Results, and all findings are tagged with the goal and
question they serve. The comparative phrasing of the final analysis is
not adopted: Section~\ref{answers} states what the evidence
supports for each dimension and makes no overall ranking.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Mapping of the GQM goals to the research questions and to the sections
that report them.}\label{tbl-rqmap}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Goal} & \textbf{Focus} & \textbf{Research question served} & \textbf{Reported in} \\
\hline
G1 & Specification and traceability & RQ1, RQ2 & Specification Structure and Traceability \\
G2 & Change-impact handling & RQ3 & Change Request CR-01 \\
G3 & Implementation and verification outcomes & RQ4 (and regressions for RQ3) & Baseline Implementation; Change Request CR-01 \\
G4 & Human work and effort & Supporting (no course RQ) & Human Work and Process Observations \\
G5 & Evidence and reproducibility & Supporting (no course RQ) & Evidence and Reproducibility \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Case and Change Request}\label{case-and-change-request}

The case is a desktop calculator in Java Swing with two operand fields,
four operation buttons, a Calculate and a Clear button, and inline error
messages. Arithmetic is exact in decimals; division rounds half-up to
ten places; results are shown as plain decimal text. The error messages
are fixed, and the error priority is empty input, then no operation,
then an invalid number, then an arithmetic error such as division by
zero. The baseline requirements were frozen on 2026-10-23. CR-01
(version 0.2) was released to both tracks on 2026-11-03 and its status
was set to Approved on 2026-11-07; the Spec Kit operator's agreement to
one of its clarification entries (LOG-16, the CR-01 status line) was
recorded on their behalf by the study owner, as the log states.

The change request, CR-01, adds three unary operations: square, square
root and reciprocal. The First number field is used and the Second
number is ignored. The square is exact; square root and reciprocal are
rounded half-up to ten places; a negative square root and a reciprocal
of zero are errors; and the criteria require all baseline checks to be
re-run. Table~\ref{tbl-case} gives the size of the case.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Size of the shared case per phase.}\label{tbl-case}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Phase} & \textbf{Requirement IDs} & \textbf{Parent acceptance criteria} & \textbf{Re-checked baseline parents} \\
\hline
Baseline & 8 functional + 5 non-functional & 19 (BASE-AC-01..19) & n/a \\
CR-01 & 11 functional (CR-FR-01..11) & 22 (CR-AC-01..22) & 19 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The case has known limits. Four baseline NFRs have no directly linked
acceptance criterion and three criteria have no requirement ID. This is
a limitation of the shared input and applies to both tracks.

\subsection{Experimental Procedure}\label{experimental-procedure}

Fig.~\ref{fig-study} shows the design. Each track started from
the same frozen requirements and acceptance criteria and kept its tool's
native workflow. The fairness rules were: the same inputs for both, no
sharing of generated artefacts between tracks, native workflows left
intact, human interventions recorded and every deviation documented.
Shared ambiguities and decisions were recorded in a common experiment
log, which both tracks had to follow. Each track froze its baseline with
a git tag before CR-01 was released. For CR-01, each track produced a
pre-coding impact analysis, implemented the change and froze the result
with a second tag. The shared baseline snapshot was
\texttt{baseline-frozen}; the BMAD change snapshot is the commit tagged
\texttt{bmad-cr01-frozen}.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio,alt={Study design: one
frozen baseline and one change request (CR-01) given to both tracks,
followed by independent evaluation.}]{figures/study-workflow.png}}
\caption{Study design: one frozen baseline and one change request
(CR-01) given to both tracks, followed by independent
evaluation.}\label{fig-study}
\end{figure}

Two members who had built neither calculator evaluated the results with
identical execution sheets for the baseline criteria and the change
criteria. One evaluated the BMAD track and one evaluated the Spec Kit
track; the Spec Kit baseline evaluation was run with assistance from an
AI coding assistant. For Spec Kit, the evaluation used the baseline
snapshot designated by the study owner, because the track's own operator
results had been recorded at an earlier commit and the track had not
declared a freeze; the log records that differences between the two
commits may exist. All sources for this report, including both tracks'
evidence and the experiment log, are in the team repository.

\subsection{Evaluation Approach and
Measures}\label{evaluation-approach-and-measures}

We report acceptance outcomes as Pass, Fail, Blocked or Not run. A
criterion counts as Pass only if the behaviour was observed. A Blocked
criterion is not a Fail, a Not run criterion is not a zero, and a later
manual completion does not replace the earlier Blocked record. Three
layers of evidence are kept apart: operator reports, independent
evaluator observations, and native automated tests. Counts of native
tests are context only, because the two tracks use different test
designs, layers and environments. Following Runeson and Höst, we
describe validity threats by category rather than hiding them in an
overall score \cite{runeson2009}, and following Kitchenham et al.~we
state counting rules explicitly \cite{kitchenham2002}.

The measures come from a catalogue of sixteen measures (M01 to M16) that
the team derived from five evaluation goals with the Goal Question
Metric approach \cite{basili1994}. Those that matter here are
acceptance outcomes (M01), native and reconstructed traceability (M03,
M13), defects (M04), regressions after the change (M06), change-impact
recall (M07), interventions and rework (M08, M12), effort and duration
(M09, M10), rubric ratings (M11) and native test inventory (M16).
Table~\ref{tbl-metrics} in the Results states which could be
calculated.

