\subsection{BMAD}\label{bmad}

BMAD, expanded by its maintainers as the Breakthrough Method for Agile
AI-Driven Development, is an open-source framework that covers deciding
what to build, how the parts fit and how the work changes as one learns
\cite{bmad}. Its documentation describes a delivery loop of clarifying
a vague idea, planning a large idea, building and verifying a small
change, and learning and adjusting. It offers specialised perspectives
for product, architecture, UX, development and testing. The installed
native flow in our track ran from an optional product brief to a PRD, an
optional UX step, an architecture, epics and stories, sprint planning,
build, and optional code review and QA.

Fig.~\ref{fig-bmad} shows the path actually followed. The
operator used a fast path for the PRD and architecture, skipped the UX
step, and had the agent judge the readiness gate. The PRD holds ten
functional and five non-functional requirements. The architecture spine
records ten architecture decisions. Work was split into three epics and
nine stories, each built with \texttt{bmad-build} and checked by three
review layers, except two stories where the operator chose a minimal
path. For the change request, \texttt{bmad-correct-course} produced a
sprint change proposal and an impact analysis before any code; after the
owner approved a new Epic 4 with four stories, the PRD, architecture and
epics were amended and the stories were built.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio,alt={BMAD as used in the
BMAD track (version 6.12.4): workflow steps, the artefact each step
leaves, and the change path.}]{figures/bmad-workflow.png}}
\caption{BMAD as used in the BMAD track (version 6.12.4): workflow
steps, the artefact each step leaves, and the change
path.}\label{fig-bmad}
\end{figure}

\subsection{GitHub Spec Kit}\label{github-spec-kit}

GitHub Spec Kit is an open-source toolkit that gives AI coding agents
structured processes, reusable templates and documented outcomes
\cite{speckit}. Its core SDD process runs in the agent's chat:
\texttt{/speckit-constitution} records project principles once,
\texttt{/speckit-specify} writes the specification,
\texttt{/speckit-plan} turns it into a technical approach,
\texttt{/speckit-tasks} derives tasks, and \texttt{/speckit-implement}
produces code; a convergence command can repeat until the result matches
the plan. The installed version also provides skills for clarification,
checklists and a read-only consistency analysis.

Fig.~\ref{fig-speckit} shows the path actually followed. The
constitution was drafted from the shared documents and amended once for
the change request (version 1.0.0 to 1.1.0) with the owner's approval.
\texttt{/speckit-specify} raised three clarification questions, which
the owner answered. The plan produced a research file, a data model,
contracts and a quickstart; the owner approved the plan before
\texttt{/speckit-tasks} generated 34 baseline tasks.
\texttt{/speckit-analyze} reported 11 findings (none critical, two high,
two medium, seven low) before any code, and fixes were applied after the
owner's approval. For CR-01 the track wrote an impact analysis first and
then ran the same chain in a new feature folder, with 49 tasks and a
second analysis of 10 findings.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio,alt={GitHub Spec Kit as
used in the Spec Kit track (specify-cli 1.2.3): commands, the artefact
each leaves, and the change path.}]{figures/speckit-workflow.png}}
\caption{GitHub Spec Kit as used in the Spec Kit track (specify-cli
1.2.3): commands, the artefact each leaves, and the change
path.}\label{fig-speckit}
\end{figure}

