re-check after CR-01, of 19.}\label{fig-acc}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Specification Structure and
Traceability}\label{specification-structure-and-traceability}

\emph{Goal G1; RQ1, RQ2.}

Both workflows produced structured, linked artefacts before coding (RQ1,
RQ2). BMAD's chain runs from the PRD and the architecture decisions to
epics, stories, tests and a requirement-to-test table. Spec Kit's runs
from the constitution and the specification to the plan, tasks, tests
and a results file with a traceability table. Both contain candidate
links from requirement to code and tests. A verified count of native
links, which would give a traceability measure (M03), has not been
established for either track, and links rebuilt afterwards by an
evaluator (M13) have to be kept apart from it. We therefore do not
report a traceability percentage.

The shared requirements were not complete enough to be implemented as
written. Both operators had to resolve ambiguities through the common
log: what happens when no operation is selected, how ties are rounded
for negative numbers, whether \texttt{.5} and \texttt{5.} are valid, and
when a shown result is cleared. Both tracks found gaps independently.
The change request also had gaps that surfaced when the BMAD track read
it, among them whether the Second number field stays visible and how an
empty input is reported. Gaps were therefore found in both phases, by
operating the workflows, not by a review of the requirements.

\subsection{Baseline Implementation}\label{baseline-implementation}

\emph{Goal G3; RQ4.}

Both operators reported all 19 baseline criteria as passed. The
independent evaluation differs (RQ4). The BMAD evaluator, who recorded
results on a reported clean checkout, reported 19 passes, but the
offline-launch criterion has no evidence that networking was disabled
and criterion-level screenshots and logs were not attached. The Spec Kit
evaluator recorded 18 passes, no failures and one Blocked criterion: the
offline launch was not executed because the network prerequisite could
not be established. That Blocked criterion is not a product failure, and
the difference between 19 and 18 reflects recorded coverage and evidence
provenance, not demonstrated correctness. The Spec Kit evaluation
retained real-window automation output, screenshots and observation
logs.

The reported examples cover the four operations, decimal precision and
rounding, negative inputs, division by zero, invalid and missing
operands, Clear, recovery after an error, sequential calculations and
the Enter key. They do not show exhaustive correctness or that every NFR
is satisfied.

\subsection{Change Request CR-01}\label{change-request-cr-01}

\emph{Goals G2 and G3; RQ3, RQ4.}

Both tracks reported all 22 change criteria as passed and all 19
baseline criteria as passed again after the change, with no regression
in the checked set (RQ3, RQ4). The two tracks' evidence is not equally
strong. The Spec Kit evaluation retained a frozen-snapshot build output,
GUI observations and screenshots, and its offline check was first
Blocked and later marked Pass through a human-reported follow-up; both
records stay visible. The BMAD evaluator's change results are recorded
in the evaluator sheets, but the exact frozen checkout and
criterion-level raw observations were not established to the same
standard. The final 22 of 22 and 19 of 19 are combined reported
statuses, not 22 independently reproduced evidence bundles.

The 22 change criteria test new behaviour and the 19 re-checks test
preserved behaviour, so the numbers do not form a series that grows from
19 to 22. As context only, the cited native test suites grew from 172 to
316 tests for BMAD (with 80 window tests skipped in a headless run and
none failing on a display) and from 81 to 116 for Spec Kit with no
failures; the two suites differ in design and cannot be compared as
quality.

Table~\ref{tbl-change} shows change handling by dimension. Both
impact analyses were useful but incomplete. The Spec Kit forecast named
five source files that were expected to change and five that were
expected to stay unchanged, and that classification held; later, work
beyond the forecast appeared: a constitution update, a changed
\texttt{OperationTest.java} and an extra helper for error-area size. The
BMAD track met GUI operation-button tests and an initial-state helper
that its analysis had not listed. A product defect, a wrong test input
or expectation and a documentation edit are different units, so counting
them against each other would mislead.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Change handling in CR-01 by dimension.}\label{tbl-change}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.300\columnwidth} p{0.300\columnwidth} p{0.300\columnwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Dimension} & \textbf{BMAD} & \textbf{Spec Kit} \\
\hline
Change planning & Story- and architecture-driven impact analysis; review layers & Impact forecast, then spec, plan and tasks \\
Unplanned effect & GUI operation-button tests; initial-state helper & Constitution update; \texttt{OperationTest.java}; error-area helper \\
Product defect found & None established in the case descriptions & Negative square-root message clipped in the GUI; found by visual review, then fixed \\
Test or documentation correction & Test input \texttt{1e41} against \texttt{1e40}; test-only review patches; one Javadoc correction & One wrong test expectation corrected \\
Impact recall (M07) & Not calculable & Not calculable \\
Rework (M12) & Candidate episodes only & Candidate episodes only \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Human Work and Process
Observations}\label{human-work-and-process-observations}

\emph{Goal G4 (supporting).}

Several observations come from the process records of single runs and
carry no statistical weight. In the BMAD track, the review layers
returned many items per story, and about half of them were verified as
false or rejected as low priority, so every item had to be triaged. The
story split by architecture layer made Epic 2 mostly a verification
exercise instead of new work. In the Spec Kit track,
\texttt{/speckit-analyze} found coverage and consistency gaps before any
code, and they were fixed after approval; the one product defect of the
change phase was found by looking at the real window, not by an
automated test.

Both tracks recorded deviations from their tool's native process, listed
in the evidence files. The BMAD track used a fast path, skipped the UX
step, did not run the PRD and architecture reviewer subagents, had the
agent judge the readiness gate, and replaced per-story approval by a
standing approval. The Spec Kit track did not run a template-resolve
step for two skills, added a ``Requirement Coverage'' table to the plan
that the tool does not produce, and left one gap, the square root of
negative zero, unspecified by the owner's decision. Both tracks also had
documentation drift: a BMAD status file still described the change as
uncommitted after it had been committed, and a Spec Kit plan referred to
a log entry before it existed.

\subsection{Evidence and
Reproducibility}\label{evidence-and-reproducibility}

\emph{Goal G5 (supporting).}

The two tracks left evidence of different depth. Both froze their
baseline and their change with git tags (\texttt{baseline-frozen},
\texttt{bmad-cr01-frozen}, \texttt{speckit-baseline-frozen},
\texttt{speckit-cr01-frozen}) and both kept evaluator sheets and an
environment record. The Spec Kit independent evaluation additionally
retained a build run on the frozen snapshot, real-window automation
output, screenshots and a dated follow-up for the offline check, so a
reader can inspect more of what the evaluator saw. The BMAD independent
evaluation recorded results in the execution sheets and screenshots, but
the exact frozen checkout and criterion-level raw observations are less
fully established. Evidence completeness (M15) was not calculated as a
percentage, and an evaluator's screenshot archive must not be credited
as the operator's own documentation. We therefore report a bounded
finding about the submitted evidence, not a general claim about either
tool.

Table~\ref{tbl-metrics} states which measures could be calculated.
Most of them could not.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Disposition of the catalogue measures in this study.}\label{tbl-metrics}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.300\columnwidth} p{0.300\columnwidth} p{0.300\columnwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Measure} & \textbf{Construct} & \textbf{Status in this study} \\
\hline
M01 & Parent acceptance outcomes & Calculable as pass ratios per phase; subcase-level evidence incomplete \\
M02 & Requirement-level status & Partial; no verified numeric status \\
M03 / M13 & Native / evaluator-reconstructed traceability & Not scored; semantic link verification pending \\
M04 / M05 & Product defects / late-defect share & Descriptive examples only; no complete census \\
M06 & Baseline regressions after CR-01 & Zero reported; observation provenance unequal \\
M07 & Change-impact omission and recall & Not calculable; no adjudicated reference set \\
M08 / M12 & Human interventions / rework episodes & Illustrative only; no comparable census \\
M09 / M10 & Active effort / elapsed duration & Not calculable; no measured sessions \\
M11 / M14 & Rubric ratings J1-J7 / reviewer agreement & Not rated; no composite score \\
M15 & Evidence completeness & Not calculable as a percentage \\
M16 & Native test inventory & Context only; no cross-track quality inference \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}\label{discussion}

\subsection{Answers to the Research Questions}\label{answers}

\textbf{RQ1 (specification structure).} The tools structure
specifications differently. BMAD builds a layered set of planning
documents (PRD, architecture decisions, epics, stories) around what the
system should do and why it is designed as it is. Spec Kit builds a
chain of principles, specification, plan and tasks around what is
specified and which tasks follow. In both, the human approved plans and
answered questions, and in both the operators had to settle ambiguities
in the shared requirements through the log. We cannot say which
structure is better; no rubric ratings of specification clarity exist.

\textbf{RQ2 (traceability).} Both produced artefacts from which a
requirement can plausibly be followed into tasks, code and tests, and
each track kept a requirement-to-test table. We could not verify native
link coverage, so the answer is limited to saying that both made links
available and that Spec Kit's coverage table was an addition to its
plan, not a tool output.

\textbf{RQ3 (change handling and alignment).} Both tracks produced a
pre-coding impact analysis, updated their planning artefacts,
implemented CR-01 and reported no regression. BMAD's path went through a
change proposal and an amended PRD, architecture and epics; Spec Kit's
through a new feature folder and an amended constitution. Neither impact
analysis was complete, and an adjudicated reference set for impact
recall does not exist, so we cannot rank them. Whether specification and
code stayed aligned was checked in both tracks only at the end of the
change, in line with the late spec-code checking that practitioners
report \cite{agarwal2026}. That observation is about our two runs; it
is not a finding about the tools.

\textbf{RQ4 (match to requirements).} Both implementations reported full
success on the shared criteria, and no independent run observed a
product failure. The independent baseline difference of one Blocked
criterion is a difference in evidence. Four NFRs have no linked
acceptance criterion, so the evaluation says little about them. Passing
19 of 19 or 22 of 22 parent criteria does not establish exhaustive
correctness.

\subsection{Observed Differences Between the
Workflows}\label{observed-differences-between-the-workflows}

We refrain from ranking the tools, but the records show three concrete
differences.

\emph{Documentation structure.} BMAD's artefacts are organised around
product and design decisions: a PRD, an architecture with numbered
decisions, epics, stories and a sprint status that tracks each story.
They explain why the design was chosen and how work was split. Spec
Kit's artefacts are organised around the specified feature: a
constitution of project principles, a specification, a plan with
research, data model and contracts, and a task list. They explain what
was specified and which tasks follow. The two document sets are not the
same kind of thing, so counting files or pages would not measure
quality, and no rubric ratings of their usefulness exist.

\emph{Change handling.} BMAD handled CR-01 as a change to an existing
plan: a sprint change proposal and impact analysis, a new Epic 4, and
amendments to the PRD, architecture and epics, with review layers on
each story. Spec Kit handled it as a new feature: an impact analysis, an
amended constitution (1.0.0 to 1.1.0), and a new feature folder with its
own specification, plan and tasks. In both, effects appeared that the
impact analysis had not listed (GUI tests and a helper for BMAD; a
constitution update, a test and a helper for Spec Kit), and neither
forecast can be scored because no reference set exists.

\emph{Independently inspectable evidence.} This is the clearest
difference in what a reader can check. The Spec Kit change evaluation
retained a frozen-snapshot build, GUI observations and screenshots, and
kept both the Blocked and the later Pass for the offline check. The BMAD
change evaluation is recorded in the evaluator sheets with less raw
evidence. The 22 of 22 and 19 of 19 results are therefore reported
statuses of unequal inspectability, not equally reproduced evidence.
This difference concerns the evidence package, not the quality of the
calculators.

Taken together, the answers are dimension-specific.
Table~\ref{tbl-strength} lists what the evidence supports and
where it stops. We make no claim about which workflow performed better
overall, handled the change better or produced better documentation,
because effort, impact recall, rework, native traceability and rubric
ratings were not measured, and observation layers differ between the
tracks.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Strengths and constraints per workflow, with evidence limits.}\label{tbl-strength}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.300\columnwidth} p{0.300\columnwidth} p{0.300\columnwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Workflow} & \textbf{Supported strengths} & \textbf{Constraints and evidence limits} \\
\hline
BMAD & Coherent PRD, architecture, epic and story organisation; review-oriented change handling; explicit impact analysis & Unplanned GUI test and helper work; test-input and documentation corrections; independent change evidence less complete \\
Spec Kit & Constitution, spec, plan and task chain; impact forecast with changed and unchanged files; retained independent build and GUI evidence; defect found and fixed & Unplanned constitution, test and helper changes; a GUI defect before correction; offline check first Blocked \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tbl-goals} restates the conclusions by measurement
goal, so that every result maps to the framework used for the
evaluation.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Conclusions by measurement goal.}\label{tbl-goals}
\centering\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth} p{0.220\columnwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Goal} & \textbf{What the evidence supports} & \textbf{What is unresolved} & \textbf{Implication} \\
\hline
G1 Specification and traceability & Both produced structured native artefacts and candidate links & Native link coverage (M03, M13) and clarity ratings & Compare mechanisms, not percentages \\
G2 Change-impact handling & Both wrote a pre-coding analysis and met unlisted effects & Impact recall (M07) needs a reference set & No ranking of impact analysis \\
G3 Implementation and verification & 22 of 22 and 19 of 19 reported by both; no reported regression & Subcase-level provenance; defect census & Similar reported outcomes, different evidence depth \\
G4 Human work and effort & Corrections and review episodes are visible & Effort, duration, rework (M08 to M10, M12) & No claim on speed or rework \\
G5 Evidence and reproducibility & Spec Kit's change evaluation retained more raw records & M15 and independent ratings (M14) & A bounded evidence advantage, not a quality score \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Answers by Measurement
Goal}\label{answers-by-measurement-goal}

The five goals of the comparison framework each pose a question about
the two workflows. We answer them here, in the same order as
Table~\ref{tbl-goals}.

\emph{G1, specification and traceability.} How clearly does each
workflow represent the shared requirements, and can they be traced
through it? Both represented them in structured native artefacts (PRD,
architecture, epics and stories for BMAD; constitution, specification,
plan and tasks for Spec Kit), and both left candidate links from
requirement to tests. How clear those artefacts are was not rated, and
native link coverage was not verified, so we describe the mechanisms and
do not score them.

\emph{G2, change-impact handling.} How does each workflow identify what
a change will affect? Both wrote an impact analysis before coding, BMAD
as a change proposal feeding a new epic and Spec Kit as a forecast of
changed and unchanged files. Both then met effects that were not listed.
Impact recall could not be computed because no reference set of affected
elements was fixed before coding.

\emph{G3, implementation and verification.} What did the implementations
achieve on the shared criteria? Both reported 22 of 22 change criteria
and 19 of 19 baseline re-checks, with no regression found. The
independent baseline results (19 for BMAD; 18 plus one Blocked for Spec
Kit) differ in evidence, not in demonstrated correctness.

\emph{G4, human work and effort.} What human decisions, effort and
rework did each workflow need? Interventions, approvals and corrections
are recorded in both tracks, but effort, duration and rework were not
measured on a common basis, so we state no difference.

\emph{G5, evidence and reproducibility.} Is the evidence enough to check
the comparison? For the change phase the Spec Kit package is easier to
inspect (frozen build, GUI observations, screenshots, a dated offline
follow-up). The BMAD package is less complete at criterion level. This
bounded difference is the main reason the reported equal outcomes cannot
be treated as equally verified.

\subsection{Implications}\label{implications}

Four practical points follow from the study, for this case and not for
all projects. A change should be judged against new behaviour and
preserved behaviour together, because the two sets of criteria answer
different questions. Planning and impact documents should be followed by
a check in the real window, because the clipped error message passed
automated tests. Keeping the original Blocked attempt next to a later
manual completion avoids hindsight bias. Finally, an independent
evaluation needs a frozen commit, the commands, timestamps and the
relevant environment state, and a measure such as impact recall needs a
prediction fixed before coding and a reference set agreed afterwards.
Effort, rework and rubric data would have to be collected while the work
is done; they cannot be reconstructed from commit times.

\subsection{Threats to Validity}\label{threats-to-validity}

\emph{Construct validity.} A parent Pass does not prove that every
subcase was independently executed, and native test counts are not
acceptance coverage. We keep these layers separate in every table.

\emph{Internal validity.} The tool, the agent and the operator are
confounded: one operator per tool, two operating systems, and a model
change at the start of the Spec Kit track. The study owner operated the
BMAD track and wrote the change request, which may bias the BMAD track
toward familiarity with the shared documents. The comparison is
descriptive and not a randomised benchmark.

\emph{Reliability.} Each workflow was run once, so we have no estimate
of the run-to-run variability of the agent. The BMAD change-phase
evaluation lacks an archived frozen checkout and raw observations of the
same depth as the Spec Kit package. The Spec Kit baseline evaluation
used a snapshot designated by the owner, not declared by the operator.
Neither track saved every prompt verbatim.

\emph{Conclusion validity.} Missing measures are not treated as zero,
and no composite score is computed. One small application, one change
request and two workflows do not support claims about SDD tools in
general.

\emph{Shared input.} The ambiguities in the baseline and in CR-01, the
four NFRs without acceptance criteria and the three criteria without
requirement IDs affect both tracks equally, but they limit what the
evaluation can show.

\section{Conclusion}\label{conclusion}

Within the limits of one case, both workflows carried the same
requirements through specification, planning, tasks, code and tests,
handled the same change request with a pre-coding impact analysis, and
reported all 22 change criteria and all 19 baseline re-checks as passed.
The independent baseline results, 19 passes for BMAD and 18 passes with
one Blocked for Spec Kit, differ in evidence provenance and not in
demonstrated correctness. Spec Kit's independent change evaluation was
more inspectable, and BMAD's planning documents gave a continuous
account of architecture and story decisions. Both required human
decisions to settle ambiguities in the shared requirements, and both met
effects of the change that their impact analyses did not list. In terms
of the measurement goals, the workflows differ in how their
documentation is organised (G1), in how the change was handled (G2) and
in how much independently inspectable evidence was retained (G5), while
the reported outcomes (G3) are alike and effort and rework (G4) were not
measured. We found no basis for an overall ranking. Future work should
fix an impact reference set before coding, measure effort and rework
prospectively, collect independent ratings, repeat the runs, and verify
native traceability link by link.

\section{AI-Use Declaration}\label{ai-use-declaration}

AI tools were both the subject of this study and a support tool for
producing it. (1) \emph{Building the calculators.} Both calculators were
built by Claude Code using the model \texttt{claude-sonnet-5-5}, acting
as each operator's coding agent; the Spec Kit track started on
\texttt{claude-opus-5-5} and switched to \texttt{claude-sonnet-5-5}. The
agent followed the BMAD 6.12.4 skills (including review subagents) and
the Spec Kit 1.2.3 skills to produce planning artefacts, code, tests and
evidence drafts. The operators set the requirements and fairness rules,
approved each plan before code was written, made and recorded the
decisions, and reviewed the outputs. (2) \emph{Requirements.} The
baseline requirements, the change request CR-01 and the acceptance
criteria were first drafted manually by the authors. ChatGPT was then
consulted to refine them. (3) \emph{Evaluation.} The Spec Kit baseline
and Spec Kit CR-01 evaluations were run by one of the authors with Codex
assistance; this is disclosed in the experiment log (LOG-23 and Section
H) and means that not every scenario was executed manually. (4)
\emph{Literature.} Source discovery and drafting of the literature
registers were AI-assisted, and an evaluation skill
(\texttt{evaluate-sdd-workflows}) was used to prepare the evaluations.
We checked every reference against its bibliographic record. For the
three preprints and the Hill et al.~paper we read only the abstract and
metadata pages, so our claims about those works are limited to what
those pages state. (5) \emph{Report.} This report was drafted and
reviewed by the authors together with Claude Code, working from the
team's repository files and from records fetched on 2026-11-06. The
authors take full responsibility for its content.

